{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1116 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7b153af23284a4a99a37cbef4892856a02792127cbecf18d94344149deae7ff2" :edb/db-before-t 1115 :edb/db-after-t 1116 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534428 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:55:14.910Z" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068491 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068491 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 2 \\sin {\\left(\\dfrac x 3\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534428 false] [17592186068491 1145 "If we let\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac x 3\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int2\\sin (\\frac{x}{3})dx &= 2\\int\\sin u \\cdot 3du \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3\\int\\sin udu \\\\\n&=-6\\cos u + C \\\\\n&=-6\\cos (\\frac{x}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 false] [17592186068491 1145 "If we let $u=\\dfrac x 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac 1 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =3\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068492 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\,  \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068492 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int 5 \\csc {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\cot {\\left(6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534428 false] [17592186068492 1145 "If we let\n\n$$\nu=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int5\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3})\\cot (6x + \\frac{π}{3})dx &= 5\\int\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3})\\cot (6x + \\frac{π}{3})dx \\\\\n&= 5\\int\\csc u\\cot u \\cdot \\frac{1}{6}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\\int\\csc u\\cot udu \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\\csc u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{6}\\csc (6x + \\frac{π}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 false] [17592186068492 1145 "If we let $u=6x+\\dfrac \\pi 3,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 6\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x =\\dfrac 1 6\\, \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068493 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139534428 false] [17592186068493 1100 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2 \\sec^2\\left(x^3-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068493 1145 "Let\n\n$$\nu = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{2}\\sec^{2} (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6})dx &= \\int\\sec^{2} (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) \\cdot x^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int\\sec^{2} (u) \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\sec^{2} (u)du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\tan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\tan (x^{3} - \\frac{π}{6}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 false] [17592186068493 1145 "Let $u = x^3 - \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 3x^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm d u = x^2\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068494 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139534428 false] [17592186068494 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/4} \\dfrac{\\tan x \\sec^2 x}{1+\\tan^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068494 1145 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nSo, let\n\n$$\nu = 1+\\tan^2 x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{\\tan x\\sec^{2} x}{1 + \\tan^{2} x}dx &= \\int_{0}^{π/4}\\frac{1}{1 + \\tan^{2} x} \\cdot \\tan x\\sec^{2} xdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(\\ln 2 - \\ln 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 false] [17592186068494 1145 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(1+\\tan^2 x) = 2\\tan{x}\\sec^2 x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u = 1+\\tan^2 x.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d} u}{\\textrm{d}x } = 2\\tan x\\sec^2 x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\tan x\\sec^2 x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi/4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1+{tan}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 true] [17592186068840 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287696 13194139534428 true] [17592186068840 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287703 13194139534428 true] [17592186068840 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287759 13194139534428 true] [17592186068840 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287766 13194139534428 true] [17592186068841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287710 13194139534428 true] [17592186068841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287745 13194139534428 true] [17592186068841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287752 13194139534428 true] [17592186068841 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287762 13194139534428 true] [17592186068842 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287724 13194139534428 true] [1759218606